---
import BaseLayout from "../components/BaseLayout.astro";
import { getSites, siteBestPower } from "../lib/sites";

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, "");

// Live totals so the prose cannot drift from the data file.
// Stated build-out is every mapped site's best-available power, running and
// proposed. Land split counts only sites that disclose an area.
let statedMw = 0;
let farmlandAcres = 0;
let brownfieldAcres = 0;
for (const f of getSites()) {
  const best = siteBestPower(f.properties);
  if (best) statedMw += best.quantity.value;
  const land = f.properties.land;
  const acres = land.acres ? land.acres.value : 0;
  if (land.land_type === "agricultural") farmlandAcres += acres;
  else if (land.land_type === "brownfield") brownfieldAcres += acres;
}
const statedGw = (statedMw / 1000).toFixed(1);

// Scale comparison — cluster vs cluster, but two different measurements.
// Yorkshire: total STATED build-out on this map (running + proposed).
// Loudoun: MEASURED county-wide data-centre power consumption (2025).
const COMPARE = [
  {
    id: "yorkshire",
    label: "Yorkshire, the Humber and Teesside, all mapped sites",
    detail: `≈${statedGw} GW stated (running + proposed)`,
    barLabel: `${statedGw} GW`,
    mw: statedMw,
    colour: "#0072B2",
    year: "2026 map",
  },
  {
    id: "loudoun",
    label: "Loudoun County, Virginia",
    detail: "5.33 GW measured cluster load (2025)",
    barLabel: "5.33 GW",
    mw: 5330,
    colour: "#E69F00",
    year: "2025",
  },
];
const compareMax = Math.max(...COMPARE.map((c) => c.mw));
const pct = (v: number, of: number) => (of > 0 ? (v / of) * 100 : 0);
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Context"
  description="How data centres across Yorkshire, the Humber and Teesside press on power, water, jobs, noise and land: sourced UK evidence, US precedents, and mitigations on record."
>
  <h1 class="font-display text-3xl m-0 tracking-tight">Context</h1>
  <p class="text-[var(--muted)] mt-2 max-w-2xl">
    The map plots sites and sourced figures. This page sets out the pressures those sites put on grids and
    communities, what US regions already show at larger scale, and mitigations already appearing in regional
    dossiers. Benefits sit next to costs. Every claim below is linked or cited; if we cannot source it, we
    cut it.
  </p>
  <p class="text-sm text-[var(--muted)] mt-3 max-w-2xl">
    Stance matches the rest of the site: disclosed, not neutral. See
    <a href={`${base}/methodology`}>methodology</a>.
  </p>

  <!-- 1. Power -->
  <section class="mt-12" id="power">
    <h2 class="font-display text-xl m-0">Power and the grid</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 max-w-2xl">
      Data centres take large, steady electricity loads. Where the local grid is already tight, new
      connections for other uses can wait years.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-3 max-w-2xl">
      <strong>Yorkshire.</strong> The corridor’s defining pattern is reuse of retired coal-power sites and
      the grid connections they leave behind:
      <a href={`${base}/site/skelton-grange`}>Skelton Grange</a> (former 800 MW coal station),
      <a href={`${base}/site/eggborough`}>Eggborough</a> (former 1,960 MW station, closed 2018), and
      <a href={`${base}/site/ferrybridge`}>Mountpark Ferrybridge</a> (former Ferrybridge C coal stock yard).
      <a href={`${base}/site/drax`}>Drax</a> states its first phase would reuse the grid connection that
      served coal generation. Where new load is added on farmland instead, it is large:
      <a href={`${base}/site/elsham`}>Elsham Tech Park</a> lists an on-site energy-centre parameter of up to
      about 49.9 MW alongside up to 1 GW of IT load (North Lincolnshire Council committee report PA/2025/643).
    </p>
    <p class="mt-3 max-w-2xl">
      <strong>US.</strong> Northern Virginia’s “Data Center Alley” is the world’s densest cluster. Loudoun
      County alone reported about <strong>5.33 GW</strong> of data-centre power consumption in 2025, up from
      about 2.0 GW in 2021, and Dominion Energy has reported tens of gigawatts under contract across its
      Virginia service area.
      (<a href="https://www.loudoun.gov/DocumentCenter/View/217605/Loudoun-County-Data-Center-Brief---Turner" rel="noopener noreferrer">Loudoun County brief, 2025</a>;
      <a href="https://www.datacenterdynamics.com/en/news/dominion-energy-nearly-doubles-data-center-capacity-under-contract-to-40gw/" rel="noopener noreferrer">DCD on Dominion’s contract pipeline, 2025</a>.)
      Read it as scale, not as identical grid conditions to the north of England.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-3 text-sm max-w-2xl">
      <strong>Mitigations on record here:</strong> reuse of existing coal-station grid connections rather
      than new greenfield ones (<a href={`${base}/site/drax`}>Drax</a>,
      <a href={`${base}/site/skelton-grange`}>Skelton Grange</a>,
      <a href={`${base}/site/eggborough`}>Eggborough</a>,
      <a href={`${base}/site/ferrybridge`}>Ferrybridge</a>), and substation plus battery storage listed in
      the reserved-matters description at <a href={`${base}/site/teesworks`}>Teesworks</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- 2. Water -->
  <section class="mt-12" id="water">
    <h2 class="font-display text-xl m-0">Water</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 max-w-2xl">
      Cooling can use large volumes of potable water. UK operators are
      <strong>not required to disclose</strong> water use, so most dossiers here show water as not disclosed,
      not as zero. Full method note: <a href={`${base}/methodology#units`}>methodology § conversion factors</a>.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-3 max-w-2xl">
      <strong>UK.</strong> An April 2026 Global Action Plan analysis reported that about <strong>84%</strong>
      of proposed UK data centres sit in areas already water-stressed or projected to be by 2040
      (<a href="https://www.watermagazine.co.uk/2026/04/27/84-of-proposed-uk-data-centres-to-be-built-in-water-stressed-areas/" rel="noopener noreferrer">Water Magazine</a>).
      Water UK has told Parliament that some proposed centres have requested up to about
      <strong>three million litres a day</strong>, and that the sector’s demand is not built into national
      water planning
      (<a href="https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2026/jul/21/not-enough-water-for-uks-datacentre-plans-trade-body-says" rel="noopener noreferrer">Guardian / Water UK, Jul 2026</a>).
    </p>
    <p class="mt-3 max-w-2xl">
      <strong>US.</strong> In The Dalles, Oregon, a public-records fight over Google’s data-centre water use
      ended in a 2022 settlement that forced disclosure; later reporting showed Google taking a large share
      of city water as campuses expanded
      (<a href="https://www.oregonlive.com/silicon-forest/2022/12/the-dalles-settles-public-records-lawsuit-over-googles-data-centers-will-disclose-water-use.html" rel="noopener noreferrer">Oregonian, Dec 2022</a>;
      <a href="https://oregoncapitalchronicle.com/2026/01/19/as-googles-water-demands-grow-the-dalles-aims-to-pull-more-from-mount-hood-forest/" rel="noopener noreferrer">Oregon Capital Chronicle, Jan 2026</a>).
      Treat these as precedents for disclosure politics, not as identical hydrology to the region.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-3 text-sm max-w-2xl">
      <strong>Mitigations on record here:</strong>
      <a href={`${base}/site/deep-green-bradford`}>Deep Green Bradford</a> claims closed-loop cooling with up
      to 95% heat reuse into the Bradford Energy Network and zero water use; air or outside-air cooling is
      claimed at <a href={`${base}/site/skelton-grange`}>Skelton Grange</a> and
      <a href={`${base}/site/wilton-international`}>Wilton International</a>. All are claims until metered.
      Waterless on site still shifts water to the power station through higher electricity use.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- 3. Jobs -->
  <section class="mt-12" id="jobs">
    <h2 class="font-display text-xl m-0">Jobs and the local economy</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 max-w-2xl">
      Promoters emphasise capital investment, business rates, and construction employment. Permanent on-site
      staffing is usually small relative to the land and power take. Site-level operational headcounts are
      not disclosed in the planning materials reviewed here, and several schemes sit inside AI Growth Zone
      bids that promise regional jobs (<a href={`${base}/methodology#aigz`}>AI Growth Zone overlay</a>).
    </p>
    <p class="mt-3 max-w-2xl">
      <strong>Benefit side.</strong> The same facilities underpin cloud and AI services used across the wider
      economy. That benefit is real. It is also spread thin: rates and construction jobs stay local, but most
      of the digital gain is used nationally. We do not score “net public value” on this site.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- 4. Noise -->
  <section class="mt-12" id="noise">
    <h2 class="font-display text-xl m-0">Noise and health</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 max-w-2xl">
      Continuous cooling plant and periodic diesel (or other) generator testing are among the impacts
      residents cite most often in objections elsewhere. Emissions from testing add an air-quality concern
      beside noise.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-3 max-w-2xl">
      <strong>US.</strong> Suburban complaints in Northern Virginia and places such as Chandler, Arizona,
      have turned on overnight noise and generator exercises. The pattern is useful: once campuses sit beside
      housing, acoustic conditions become a planning lever. No noise objection is yet on record in the
      dossiers mapped here.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-3 text-sm max-w-2xl">
      <strong>Mitigations:</strong> planning conditions on plant enclosure, hours for generator testing,
      acoustic barriers, and (where proposed) HVO or electric backup. Check each dossier’s planning documents
      rather than assuming a regional standard.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- 5. Land -->
  <section class="mt-12" id="land">
    <h2 class="font-display text-xl m-0">Land</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 max-w-2xl">
      The build-out here splits between brownfield reuse of retired coal-power sites
      (<a href={`${base}/site/skelton-grange`}>Skelton Grange</a>,
      <a href={`${base}/site/eggborough`}>Eggborough</a>,
      <a href={`${base}/site/ferrybridge`}>Ferrybridge</a>,
      <a href={`${base}/site/teesworks`}>Teesworks</a>,
      <a href={`${base}/site/aql-leeds`}>aql Leeds</a>) and farmland schemes on the fringe
      (<a href={`${base}/site/elsham`}>Elsham</a>, <a href={`${base}/site/humber-tech-park`}>Humber Tech Park</a>).
      Across sites that disclose an area, farmland accounts for about {farmlandAcres} acres and ex-industrial
      land about {brownfieldAcres}. Brownfield-first is the usual mitigation in policy language; loss of
      farmland, especially best and most versatile land, is decided case by case.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-3 max-w-2xl" id="land-question">
      <strong>The land question.</strong> A consent commits a piece of land to one long-term use. A scheme
      can turn working farmland into a fenced, single-use site that takes large power and water; how many
      permanent local jobs it supports is not disclosed in the planning materials here
      (<a href="#jobs">jobs</a>). Elsham Tech Park would permanently take over 430 acres, roughly 140
      hectares of it best and most versatile farmland (grades 1, 2 and 3a), for up to 1 GW of IT load
      (North Lincolnshire Council committee report PA/2025/643). Once built it is hard to undo; a hall of
      racks and cooling plant does not go back to being a field.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-3 max-w-2xl" id="rival-claims">
      <strong>Rival claims on scarce capacity.</strong> Land, grid headroom, and water are shared resources,
      and a data centre’s claim on each one competes with other uses. At
      <a href={`${base}/site/teesworks`}>Teesworks</a> the data-centre campus land overlapped BP’s
      H2Teesside hydrogen scheme; both were framed as nationally significant until BP withdrew H2Teesside in
      December 2025
      (<a href="https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cn43x9042kwo" rel="noopener noreferrer">BBC</a>).
      Farmland, once consented, is spent: the objections at
      <a href={`${base}/site/elsham`}>Elsham</a> turn on the permanent loss of productive best-and-most-versatile
      land (PA/2025/643).
    </p>
    <p class="mt-3 max-w-2xl" id="who-carries-the-cost">
      <strong>Who carries the cost.</strong> The local costs concentrate: the farmland itself, the noise, the
      traffic, and sometimes water use fall on the immediate area. Most of the benefit is national: the cloud
      and AI capacity these sites host is used across the economy, not mainly nearby
      (<a href="#jobs">jobs</a>). Opponents call this a land-justice problem.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-3 max-w-2xl">
      <strong>Benefits.</strong> The strongest case here is the brownfield route. Reusing retired coal-power
      land — <a href={`${base}/site/skelton-grange`}>Skelton Grange</a>,
      <a href={`${base}/site/eggborough`}>Eggborough</a>, and
      <a href={`${base}/site/ferrybridge`}>Ferrybridge</a> — puts derelict industrial sites and their
      existing grid connections back to work, and raises no farmland question at all. Clustering compute near
      that infrastructure can also lower connection cost.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- 6. Yorkshire vs US -->
  <section class="mt-12" id="yorkshire-vs-us">
    <h2 class="font-display text-xl m-0">How the region compares with the US</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 max-w-2xl">
      Single US AI and cloud campuses are often quoted in the <strong>multi-gigawatt</strong> range, and
      Loudoun County’s measured cluster load alone was about <strong>5.33 GW in 2025</strong>. The whole
      mapped build-out here, running plus proposed, is about <strong>{statedGw} GW</strong> stated; its
      largest single figures are around <strong>1 GW</strong> (Elsham’s IT-load parameter and Drax’s
      longer-term ambition), and both are stated ceilings, not consented running loads. The region is smaller
      per site and more constrained on farmland and grid headroom, so friction such as farmland loss, grid
      reuse, and land conflicts like Teesworks shows up at lower absolute megawatts.
    </p>

    <div class="mt-6 space-y-4" role="img"
      aria-label={`Scale comparison: the region's whole mapped build-out about ${statedGw} gigawatts stated; Loudoun County 2025 measured cluster load 5.33 gigawatts.`}>
      {COMPARE.map((row) => (
        <div>
          <div class="flex flex-wrap items-baseline justify-between gap-2 text-sm">
            <p class="m-0 font-medium">{row.label}</p>
            <p class="m-0 font-tabular text-[var(--muted)]">
              {row.barLabel}<span class="text-xs"> · {row.year}</span>
            </p>
          </div>
          <p class="m-0 mt-0.5 text-xs text-[var(--muted)]">{row.detail}</p>
          <div class="mt-2 h-8 rounded overflow-hidden bg-[var(--line)]">
            <div
              class="h-full flex items-center px-2 text-xs font-medium text-white whitespace-nowrap"
              style={`width:${Math.max(pct(row.mw, compareMax), 12)}%;background:${row.colour}`}
            >
              {row.barLabel}
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>

    <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-[var(--muted)] max-w-2xl">
      Metric caution: both sides are cluster-scale, but they are not the same measurement. The regional figure
      is the stated total of every mapped site, running and proposed. Loudoun’s is measured power actually
      consumed across the county cluster in 2025. Read it as scale, not a like-for-like league table.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="mt-12 border-t border-[var(--line)] pt-8">
    <h2 class="font-display text-xl m-0">Use the map</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-sm max-w-2xl">
      <a href={`${base}/`}>Map</a>
      · <a href={`${base}/sites`}>Sites</a>
      · <a href={`${base}/compare`}>Compare</a>
      · <a href={`${base}/research`}>Research</a>
      · <a href={`${base}/methodology`}>Methodology</a>
      · <a href={`${base}/data/sites.geojson`}>GeoJSON</a>
    </p>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
